.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt BUILTIN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm builtin ,
.Nm alias ,
.Nm bg ,
.Nm cd ,
.Nm command ,
.Nm fc ,
.Nm fg ,
.Nm getopts ,
.Nm hash ,
.Nm jobs ,
.Nm read ,
.Nm type ,
.Nm ulimit ,
.Nm umask ,
.Nm unalias ,
.Nm wait
.Nd shell built-in commands
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm builtin
.Ar name
.Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These commands are built into the shell, which documents them:
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr zsh 1 .
.Pp
.Nm builtin
runs the shell's built-in
.Ar name
even when a function or a file of that name comes first.
.Pp
POSIX also asks for
.Nm alias ,
.Nm bg ,
.Nm cd ,
.Nm command ,
.Nm fc ,
.Nm fg ,
.Nm getopts ,
.Nm hash ,
.Nm jobs ,
.Nm read ,
.Nm type ,
.Nm ulimit ,
.Nm umask ,
.Nm unalias
and
.Nm wait
to be found as files, so that programs which start commands without a
shell, such as
.Xr env 1 ,
.Xr find 1
.Fl exec
and
.Xr xargs 1 ,
can run them.
Each file in
.Pa /usr/bin
runs
.Xr sh 1
with its built-in of the same name.
.Pp
Those which change the shell's own state
.Pq Nm cd , Nm umask , Nm alias , Nm read
have no lasting effect when run as a file: they act in a shell that ends
when they do.
They still report, through their exit status, whether they would succeed;
.Ql find . -type d ! -exec cd {} \e; -print
lists the directories that cannot be entered.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/bin/alias -compact
.It Pa /usr/bin/alias
the script behind each name
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr zsh 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The files follow
.St -p1003.1-2008 ,
which lists these utilities as regular built-ins.
